      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49579812",
      "why_read": "This text explains how an adversarial, multi-agent review process can improve the quality of coding agent solutions. It highlights the pitfalls of self-review and offers a structured approach for independent verification.",
      "authors": [
        "alpbahadur"
      ],
      "content": "Most coding agents are confidently wrong. Their initial solutions are often plausible but incomplete, leading to subtle bugs you only discover in production. Simply asking the same agent to \"double-check\" rarely works, as it operates with the same blind spots.\n\nThis \"interns-review\" pattern flips the script. Instead of self-correction, it deploys 1-3 independent LLM \"interns\" to conduct an adversarial review of the lead agent's design choices. These reviewers get a clean context, free from the lead agent's assumptions, allowing them to objectively tear apart the proposed solution.\n\nThe lead agent then acts as a discerning judge, verifying findings against actual code and rejecting unverified or irrelevant suggestions. This multi-agent, adversarial approach is a significant step forward for building more robust and reliable AI agents. It addresses the core problem of agent overconfidence by injecting critical, independent thought into the development loop."
